  <article class="note">
      <p>One of the initial thoughts that I had when we saw the increased use of mainstream LLMs was various subtle shifts we were going to see, because the way we interact with information was having a fundamental shift. These shifts include how propaganda flows in politics, how companies market their products and also maybe in the type of content we consume. There are some behavioral aspects also, such as the reduction in peer-to-peer learning, or even basic work becoming so easy we may lose some basis points in general intelligence – these are all thoughts that only time will answer. While these are topics I will certainly do a deep dive into at some point, especially the behavioral angle, I have also been meaning to explore how financial systems may shift as LLMs become more entrenched.</p>
      <p>When I sat down to think about this, there were a couple of angles that I thought could be genuinely useful to look at. One was just the increased access to information and capability – most people now have a pocket analyst that can do research for them, and they just have to ask the right questions. Maybe how it is now easier to build quant models, and fundamental, or short-term traders can leverage their information to develop a deeper edge with tech systems. But the one that stood out to me the most was: <strong>how LLMs </strong><strong>could</strong><strong> shift how people and companies interact with the security </strong><strong>markets</strong><strong>. </strong></p>
      <p>Now what I mean by this is that more people are starting to rely on these models to help them make decisions with their money. You’re going to have individuals more comfortable making investments because frontier model research comes with the guise that you have researched and are making an informed decision. I believe we may see an increase in the sheer quantity of people that enter the markets as a result of this. While it is intuitive to understand on a personal basis, because we’ve all asked ChatGPT for some investing tips, it’s worth exploring how we are seeing this style of advice being baked into our systems on an institutional level as well.</p>
      <p>This was one of the topics of discussions in one of the GFF panels – ‘Reimagining the Advice stack with AI’. The premise around the discussion was we are now able to reach a much wider audience when it comes to financial advice, because you can have smarter models that are able to automate large parts of the advice. Imagine something like a like a Google Pay chatbot that helps in managing investments and spending. Or more sophisticated products from wealth management firms that automate PMS service, and have automated RMs for the lowest rung of net worth where the unit economics are too thin to have a human RM. This automated advisory layer has blown up over the last 6 months, firms like Dezerv, 1Finance etc. have products out, and I have come across reports by BCG, Mckenzie, LSEG exploring how AI can be <strong>used </strong>in wealth management.</p>
      <p>This shift is a very natural one, as capabilities get more sophisticated, you are going to see it extended to the masses, because if you are able to capture this Indian market, you have a golden goose. But I am not here to vet the business alignment of this, I want to understand what the impact this may have down the line.</p>
      <p>I managed to have a couple of discussions with the panelists post their talk, and they did echo this concern, but it was a bit muted as they didn’t think the impact may be as widespread. In fairness this was just their initial thoughts when a random guy approaches them with a question, so I believe it isn’t something that has been mulled over. So, I decided to mull on it myself, and this is what I think.</p>
      <p>The first shift I see is that we may see a shift in the way that money moves from retail investors into these instruments. This is because while the process is going to be entirely automated, these will be human created systems, with weights, biases and preferences for a certain type of investment.  Now the issue I have with the automation of this type of advice, is that I fear it would lead to a very binary flow of good and bad investments or stocks, and we could see a concentration of funds in certain places. What I mean that is a model will inevitably have some fixed parameters of what a good stock pick is (textbook patterns) and then use that information to suggest a rebalancing or even opportunity to a customer. And when you have this over a significant amount of people, like the wealth management industry wants to do, you’ll have large sums of money flowing into certain pockets. Popular stocks and mutual funds are going to see an increase in demand.</p>
      <p>I think there's two distinct ways it plays out. The first is a straightforward valuation effect, when the same handful of names keep clearing the screen across millions of portfolios, they get bid up past what their fundamentals justify, not because the business has improved but because the model keeps pointing people toward it. The second is a correlation effect, once a large chunk of retail money is being steered by a similar set of models toward a similar set of names, those stocks start moving together in a way they didn't before, so any shift in what the model treats as a good pick, whether from a training update or a change in sentiment, could trigger correlated selling across portfolios that otherwise have nothing in common.</p>
      <p>While there is certainly a substantial impact, I want to offer a little bit of balance in this take and highlight a different conversation I had with one of the panelists; whether there is even going to be a concentrated pool like I imagine. The basic idea of the rebut is that the advice is bound to be personalized.</p>
      <p>The basic advice on what to do with one’s money, or which stock may be the best pick would depend on a variety of modelled personal factors, that may vary person to person. Aspects such as portfolio size, age, investment horizon, risk appetite would all bring the necessary nuance. And if this is the case, I do agree, we may not be seeing flows like I visualized.</p>
      <p>The questions then shift to how firms model this model these aspects well and thus have a nuanced advisory offering. So, while the flow of money may not be as concentrated as I imagined, I still believe with automated advice to lots of people will shift it towards this direction. Because there is only so much nuance you can model, there is only so much context people will give you, and then in that case the advice starts becoming increasingly similar across people. But this only time will tell.</p>
      <p>While we have touched on how individual decision may shift with the use of LLMs in the advice stack, I want to shed some light on another angle that this advisory stack may impact: <strong>f</strong><strong>irms and companies trying to reverse engineer the way they are picked up by these LLMs. </strong></p>
      <p>Like I touched upon earlier, this is very common with how products are being advertised now. You have SEO and GEO firms seeing considerable success, reverse engineering how to be the first option on ChatGPT when you search ‘best shampoo brands. But I fear this may extend to our financial markets as well. Once companies become cognizant that a lot of mainstream investment decisions are to be automated, they are bound to start making small tweaks to picked up favorably. Now, there are a plethora of ways they can do this, news, announcement, maybe even decisions. But I think the most direct one, and something that we will certainly see is the slight shift in annual reports. When you have an automated screening process, some ratios, accounting entries will be given a higher preference than others. So naturally you’re going to see companies try and rebalance their accounts. As a thought exercise, here is three ways I would do it if I was at a large listed company:</p>
      <ol class="exercise">
        <li><p>If leverage is the ratio getting screened, I'd move part of a loan into a joint venture I don't fully consolidate. The debt is still technically still the companies, but on paper it now sits a step below where the model is looking, and my ratio comes in clean without the underlying picture changing at all.</p></li>
        <li><p>Now when it comes to improving margins, I'd start capitalising costs that are run straight through the P&amp;L, things like development spend or certain overheads. The cash position wouldn’t have moved, but EBITDA ticks up a few points every quarter, so an LLM screen would catch it first</p></li>
        <li><p>This is where I think the most leverage can be created; if I know a model is reading my sentiments, I'd keep the language in my earnings call and my MD&amp;A flat and consistent quarter after quarter. Try and keep the same confidence no matter what's actually going on. I’d try to let anything genuinely uncertain sit further down the filing so it’s less likely to be picked up by an automated screen</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p>If you think this is a far-fetched idea, companies don’t have incentive to play around with their accounts and annual reports, you’d be very wrong. This has been the case for years, companies have always padded, kicked the can down, and even hidden various transactions and details just so they can show better results in certain quarters. I remember reading about this two years ago in a book by Howard M. Schilit called ‘Financial Shenanigans’ and now we have a new way for the markets to be steered from glass cabins in BKC.</p>
      <p>While this is coming from the lens of a wealth advice stack and change how advisory LLMs pick stocks, it could just affect how companies screen stocks in general. Most analyst and firms use Claude to read, parse and even make decisions on various companies, yes as a preliminary screen, but this could start causing a bias at layer one.</p>
      <p>Now, unlike the previous writeup’s, there is no real story or a definitive result of what is going to happen next, but I wanted to use this as an example of systems are going to see a subtle shift and the more aware you are off it, the better. If you think that the markets are going to have a concentration risk at increased valuations (which it already does, but I digress), then knowing how to identify undervalued stocks has an important nuance. If companies do start tweaking their annual reports and accounts to be looked at more favorably, then it becomes incumbent to know how to look out for these, which means also to understand the system that is being reverse engineered. Just remember that once information starts having a more concentrated source, and one that can be gamed, the ability to separate genuine from biased and good from bad becomes the responsibility of the consumer.</p>
  </article>
